  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Mainly dry conditions with variable amounts of \nhigh based stratocu and altocu clouds will persist through \nSaturday with any precipitation chances confined to the southern\ntier of the state.\n\nA northwesterly upper level jet core will extend from southern \nOntario to the New England Coast. As the right entrance region \nof this jet drifts over western and southern Pennsylvania this \nafternoon it will interact with a remnant MCV from convection \nthat is currently ongoing across Illinois and Indiana.\n\nThis area of more focused ascent should lead to another round \nof clouds and some shower development in the southern tier of \nthe state later today.\n\nMain timing looks to be from the late morning to mid-afternoon. \nThe ascent may allow for some spotty afternoon showers \nelsewhere, but dry air filtering in from the north should keep \nthings fairly isolated. \n\nIn the wake of upper level features, drier air filters in from \nthe north and skies should start clearing this evening into \nSaturday. Noticeably less humid conditions will accompany the \nstart of the weekend with any isolated rain chances looking to \nremain in the southwest. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Sunday will bring a return to wet conditions \nwith showers and thunderstorm chances returning. \n\nA low pressure system moving through Canada will allow a stalled\nfrontal boundary to move through Pennsylvania as a warm front \non Sunday. This will bring a moist airmass, with PWATs possibly \nexceeding 2\" into Pennsylvania. With a secondary low likely \nforming closer to Pennsylvania at the intersection of the warm \nfront and a trailing cold front, this will invigorate \ndevelopment of showers and thunderstorms. With a cold front \ndraping across Pennsylvania Sunday night, multiple rounds of \nshowers/thunderstorms may be possible into the night. \n\nAs it stands right now, ensemble guidance has the northern tier\nof Pennsylvania targeted for highest rain probabilities and\ntotals. Exact timing of this activity and how fast/slow the \ncold front moves through will likely become clearer as we \napproach Sunday. With such high PWATs and potential for multiple\nrounds/training activity, flooding potential will need to be \nwatched. Should the cold front stall/move slower, activity may \nlinger into Monday morning. After that passage, the start of \nnext week is showing signs of high pressure moving over the \nregion.",
